[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_dist_2_1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF adjacency between distribution switch switch_dist_2_1 and core router router_core_2. The OSPF process on switch_dist_2_1 is misconfigured on its uplink interface.

Mechanism: OSPF neighbors only form when key hello parameters (including Area ID) match. The uplink between switch_dist_2_1 (172.16.0.9/31 on eth0) and router_core_2 (172.16.0.8/31 on eth2) is in different OSPF areas on each end, so hellos are discarded and the adjacency never progresses beyond Down, despite the link being physically up. With no OSPF neighbor, switch_dist_2_1 does not learn inter-area routes, so its SVI 10.2.1.1 (gateway for host_2_1_1_1) cannot forward traffic to pod 1 or the server farm.

Localization and evidence:
- switch_dist_2_1 has no OSPF neighbors (show ip ospf neighbor returns header only).
- switch_dist_2_1 OSPF config: “network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0” while its access SVI is “area 1” (frr_show_running_config).
- router_core_2 OSPF config: “network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1” on eth2 (frr_show_running_config); its neighbor list lacks the eth2 peer.
- Interface IPs confirm the link: switch_dist_2_1 eth0 172.16.0.9/31, router_core_2 eth2 172.16.0.8/31 (get_host_net_config).

Root cause: An OSPF area mismatch was introduced on switch_dist_2_1 during the change window, placing the 172.16.0.8/31 uplink in area 0 instead of area 1.

Corrective fix: On switch_dist_2_1, change the OSPF statement to “network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1” (to match router_core_2), write mem, and clear OSPF neighbors or restart FRR. The adjacency should come up and inter-area reachability for 10.2.1.0/24 will be restored. === END ===